 ("h2", "1.3 Seeing Patterns with Pictures"),
 ("h3", "Square numbers"),
 ("p", "A **square number** is the number of dots in a square arrangement: 1 × 1 = 1, 2 × 2 = 4, 3 × 3 = 9, and so on. We write it as n² (read ‘n squared’). The 12th square number is 12 × 12 = 144."),
 ("h3", "Triangular numbers"),
 ("p", "A **triangular number** is the number of dots when rows of 1, 2, 3, … dots are stacked to form a triangle. The 4th triangular number is 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 = 10 (like ten bowling pins). The rule for the 1st, 2nd, 3rd … terms is 1, 1 + 2, 1 + 2 + 3, …"),
 ("note", "FORMULA", "The nth triangular number = 1 + 2 + 3 + … + n = **n × (n + 1) ÷ 2**.  For example, 1 + 2 + … + 20 = 20 × 21 ÷ 2 = **210**, and 1 + 2 + … + 100 = 100 × 101 ÷ 2 = **5050**."),
 ("p", "**Why does the formula work?** Write the sum twice, once forwards and once backwards. Each pair of numbers one above the other adds to the same total (n + 1), and there are n pairs, so twice the sum is n × (n + 1). Half of that is the answer. This is the ‘pairing’ trick: 1 + 100 = 101, 2 + 99 = 101, … fifty pairs, so 50 × 101 = 5050."),
